From a2ac97efa45ce86936f0242537bf5363185fbeeb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 15:38:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos7870-gtaxlwifi: keep LDO31 on for the GPS

LDO31 at 2.8 V supplies the BCM4752 GPS, and no driver asks for it. The
vendor kernel describes it boot-on and always-on, and so did the U-Boot
this tablet booted with, which turned it on; S-BOOT leaves it off. Since
U-Boot gave way to a loader that does not touch the PMIC, the chip never
answered on its UART ("ASIC communication failure during stage HAL
init"), and the regulator could not be turned on by Linux either, being
undescribed.

Describe it as the vendor does. Tested on the tablet: the UART receives
again, and outdoors the first fix comes after 95.6 s cold, 9 of 11
satellites used, 12 m accuracy.

---
 .../boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-gtaxlwifi.dts      | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-gtaxlwifi.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-gtaxlwifi.dts
index b926a0f1d7..f1c6e73e16 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-gtaxlwifi.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos7870-gtaxlwifi.dts
@@ -1275,6 +1275,21 @@ vdd_ldo34: ldo34 {
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 			};
 
+			/*
+			 * LDO31 at 2.8 V supplies the BCM4752 GPS. No driver asks
+			 * for it: the vendor kernel and the U-Boot this tablet
+			 * booted with both keep it on (boot-on, always-on), and
+			 * S-BOOT leaves it off. Without it the chip never answers
+			 * on its UART.
+			 */
+			vdd_ldo31: ldo31 {
+				regulator-name = "vdd_ldo31";
+				regulator-min-microvolt = <2800000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <2800000>;
+				regulator-boot-on;
+				regulator-always-on;
+			};
+
 			vdd_ldo33: ldo33 {
 				regulator-name = "vdd_ldo33";
 				regulator-min-microvolt = <2800000>;
-- 
